## Magic Square Problem in Minizinc

### Problem Description

The problem is to find a magic square of size 4, where each cell contains a different integer from 1 to 16, and the sum of the integers in each row, column, and diagonal is equal.

### Minizinc Model

```minizinc
int: n = 4;

% Define the size of the grid
array[1..n, 1..n] of var 1..n*n: square;

% Calculate the expected sum for rows, columns, and diagonals
int: expected_sum = n * (n*n + 1) div 2;

% Constraint: All cells must be different
constraint all_different([square[i, j] | i in 1..n, j in 1..n]);

% Constraint: Sum of each row is equal to the expected sum
constraint forall (i in 1..n) (
    sum([square[i, j] | j in 1..n]) = expected_sum
);

% Constraint: Sum of each column is equal to the expected sum
constraint forall (j in 1..n) (
    sum([square[i, j] | i in 1..n]) = expected_sum
);

% Constraint: Sum of the main diagonal is equal to the expected sum
constraint sum([square[i, i] | i in 1..n]) = expected_sum;

% Constraint: Sum of the anti-diagonal is equal to the expected sum
constraint sum([square[i, n-i+1] | i in 1..n]) = expected_sum;

% Solve the problem
solve satisfy;

% Output the solution as a list of lists
output [
    "\"square\": [",
    join(",\n", [
        "[" ++ join(",", [show(square[i, j]) | j in 1..n]) ++ "]"
        | i in 1..n
    ]),
    "]"
];
```

